.TH PASSWD 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
passwd \- change a user's login password
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B passwd
.RI [ user ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B passwd
changes the password stored in
.I /etc/shadow
for
.IR user ,
or for the user owning the real user ID of the calling process when no
.I user
is given.
The user must exist in the password database
.RB ( getpwnam (3)).
Only the superuser (real user ID 0) may change another user's password;
any further arguments are ignored.
.PP
A caller other than the superuser is first prompted for the current
password, which is checked against the stored hash with
.BR crypt (3).
If the stored password field is empty, only an empty entry is accepted.
The superuser is not asked for the current password.
.PP
The new password is then read twice.
The two entries must match and must not be empty.
Passwords are read from standard input one byte at a time up to a
newline or carriage return; at most 127 bytes are kept.
When standard input is a terminal, echo is turned off while a password
is typed, and the previous terminal settings are restored if
.BR SIGINT ,
.BR SIGQUIT ,
or
.B SIGTERM
arrives during the prompt (the program then exits with status 128 plus
the signal number).
.PP
The new password is hashed with SHA-256-crypt: a
.B $5$
setting with an 8-character salt taken from
.I /dev/urandom
and the default number of rounds.
Existing MD5-crypt
.RB ( $1$ )
or DES hashes still verify and are replaced by a
.B $5$
hash when the password is changed.
If eight random bytes cannot be read, the change is refused rather than
using a weaker salt.
.PP
The shadow file is updated while holding the password-database lock
.RI ( /etc/.pwd.lock )
using
.BR pwdb_atomic_rewrite (3):
a new file is written to
.IR /etc/shadow.new ,
synced, given the mode and owner of the old file, and renamed over
.IR /etc/shadow .
Only the second field of the user's line is replaced.
If the user has no line in
.IR /etc/shadow ,
the file is left untouched and the change fails.
Buffers that held passwords or hashes are overwritten before exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The password was changed.
.TP
.B 1
The user does not exist, permission was denied, authentication failed,
a password could not be read, the new passwords did not match or were
empty, or
.I /etc/shadow
could not be updated.
.TP
.B "128 + signal"
The program was interrupted during a password prompt.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/passwd
User database, consulted through
.BR getpwnam (3)
and
.BR getpwuid (3).
.TP
.I /etc/shadow
Password hashes; read to verify the current password and rewritten.
.TP
.I /etc/shadow.new
Temporary file used during the rewrite.
It is created exclusively; if a stale copy is left behind, the update
fails until it is removed.
.TP
.I /etc/.pwd.lock
Advisory lock shared with the other password-database tools.
.TP
.I /dev/urandom
Source of the salt.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR id (1),
.BR crypt (3),
.BR pwdb (3),
.BR pwdb_atomic_rewrite (3),
.BR pwdb_lock (3),
.BR useradd (8),
.BR usermod (8)
.SH BUGS
The root filesystem build does not install
.B passwd
set-user-ID root, so a user other than the superuser cannot take the
lock or replace
.I /etc/shadow
and gets
.RB \(dq "failed to update /etc/shadow" \(dq.
.PP
Input longer than 127 bytes is truncated silently, and the unread
remainder of the line is consumed by the next prompt.
